# Model policy (super workflow)
- Orchestrator: strong tier, always (`"model": "opus"`, set by setup in the account's settings.json (`$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/settings.json`, default `~/.claude/settings.json`)).
- Executors: fast tier first. `/orchestrate` passes `model: haiku` unless Jev's `needs_sonnet` or `needs_opus` (or strong-tier judgment) starts the task higher; the plugin's `dryas:executor` agent stays on the standard tier so review dispatches never run on the fast tier. Kill switch: `dispatch.haiku_default` in `~/.dryas/jev/thresholds.json`.
- Escalation ladder: fast → standard → strong → frontier (tier-to-model table in `~/.dryas/docs/dryas-workflow.md`). A task the fast tier fails `escalation.haiku_failures_before_sonnet` times (default 1) goes to the standard tier; a task the standard tier can't finish goes to a strong executor; the frontier tier is used only if the strong executor also fails that task. Stop at the first success. Every step is logged with its reason via `mcp__plugin_dryas_jev__log_escalation`.
- Jev (via the `jev` MCP server and hooks) answers narrow typed questions only; it never writes code or plans. Below threshold or escalate → the strong tier decides.

# Compact instructions
- When compacting, keep the summary SHORT (aim under 300 words): current goal, open decisions, next step. A Jev-scored handoff of verbatim excerpts (user messages, files touched, test results, commits, key tool lines) is re-injected right after compaction, so do not repeat file lists, test output or tool logs in the summary.
